    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "12.2: A Tangent Ratio",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Where do you think the name “tangent” of an angle comes from?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Before we graph \\(y= \\tan(\\theta)\\), let’s figure out some things that must be true.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Explain why the graph of \\(\\tan(\\theta)\\) has a vertical asymptote at \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Does the graph of \\(\\tan(\\theta)\\) have other vertical asymptotes? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For which values of \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\tan(\\theta)\\) zero? For which values of \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\tan(\\theta)\\) one? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Is the graph of \\(\\tan(\\theta)\\) periodic? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The tangent of an angle \\(\\theta\\), \\(\\tan(\\theta)\\), is the quotient of the sine and cosine: \\(\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sin(\\theta)}{\\cos(\\theta)}\\). Here is a graph of \\(y = \\tan(\\theta)\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can see from the graph that \\(\\tan(\\theta) = 0\\) when \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\text-2\\pi, \\text-\\pi, 0, \\pi, \\text{or } 2\\pi\\). This makes sense because the sine is 0 for these values of \\(\\theta\\). Since sine and cosine are never 0 at the same…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can also see the asymptotes of tangent \\(\\text-\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\text-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\text{and }\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\). Let’s look more closely at what happens when \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). We have \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 1\\)…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Like the sine and cosine functions, the tangent function is periodic. This makes sense because it is defined using sine and cosine. The period of tangent is only \\(\\pi\\) while the period of sine and cosine is \\(2\\pi\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L12-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: The Tangent Function",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function whose values repeat at regular intervals. If \\(f\\) is a periodic function then there is a number \\(p\\), called the period, so that \\(f(x + p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
